e-work/\" id=\"24zFMCBp37jAKAiDj3EwwV\"><div class=\"relative w-full text-primary-100\"><div class=\"mb-4 text-h5 @md:pe-8\">Work at the Frontier: AI’s effect on freelance work</div><p class=\"flex flex-wrap gap-x-3 gap-y-2 text-meta @md:pe-3\"><span>Company</span><span class=\"flex flex-wrap gap-x-3 gap-y-2\"><time class=\"text-nowrap text-primary-60\" datetime=\"2026-11-10T07:00\">Nov 10, 2026</time></span></p></div></a></div></div></div></div></div></div></article>",
  "body_text": "<p>20,000 veterans are using ChatGPT to build what comes nextIn the past year, more than 20,000 transitioning servicemembers and veterans have claimed a year ofChatGPT Plus at no costafter being offered free access by OpenAI. That milestone gives us a chance to look at how veterans are using AI to prepare for civilian careers and to figure out what comes next for themselves and their families.</p><p>The effort started with veterans who work here at OpenAI, people who use these tools every day and know firsthand how challenging the transition can be. Beyond the free access, OpenAI Academy and our community partners have hosted more than a dozen trainings for nearly 1,000 members of the military community,</p><p>with seven morescheduled before the year ends.Preparing for civilian careers</p><p>Leaving the military often means stepping into an unfamiliar job market and working out how years of service translate into a civilian role. Many veterans are turning to ChatGPT to organize their job search, rehearse for interviews, and explore paths they hadn’t considered.</p><p>Learning to use ChatGPT, together</p><p>Not everyone arrives confident about using AI tools, which is why OpenAI Academy gives veterans, transitioning servicemembers, and military spouses a place to learn alongside people who understand what they’re going through.</p><p>OpenAI recently wrapped our first six-event Academy series withVeterans Forge, focused on helping veterans and military families use ChatGPT to work toward their goals. That pilot, together with workshops alongside AI Collective Hampton Roads and NPower, is shaping what we offer veterans next.</p><p>At oneworkshop in Reston, Virginia, nearly100 veterans and active-duty servicemembersexplored ways to put AI to work. ParticipantRyan Kaono-Wedemeyerlearned to start by writing out a detailed account of his military experience, then asking AI to help shape it into a civilian résumé.InstructorErin Mariehelped participants build custom GPTs to support their transitions, and watched people who walked in skeptical begin finding ways the tools could genuinely help them.</p><p>In Louisville, Kentucky,more than 80 Legionnairesattended three AI training sessions at the American Legion’s national convention. For those who can’t make it to an event, ouronline veterans collectionand100 chats for veteransoffer examples to try on your own.What’s nextWe’re continuing this work with partners across the country through the rest of the year:</p><p>November 12:NPower event in St. LouisNovember 14:Nashville event with Veterans Forge and Wounded Warrior ProjectNovember 17:30-minute virtual sessionNovember 19:NPower event in DallasNovember 21:NPower event in San AntonioOur goal is to help veterans and military families carry the skills they built in service into whatever work they choose next. We’re grateful toID.mefor helping servicemembers and veterans access the free-year offer, and to our community partners for making these events possible.This Veterans Day, OpenAI thanks veterans for their service and for contributing to their communities.</p><p>See if you’re eligible for a free year of ChatGPT Plus.</p><h2>Author</h2><h2>Keep reading</h2>",
